WEBVTT

1
00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:57.015
Before time began, there was the Cube.

2
00:00:57.516 --> 00:00:59.685
We know not where it comes from,

3
00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:06.024
only that it holds the power
to create worlds and fill them with life.

4
00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:09.611
That is how our race was born.

5
00:01:09.695 --> 00:01:12.197
For a time, we lived in harmony,

6
00:01:12.239 --> 00:01:18.870
but like all great power,
some wanted it for good, others for evil.

7
00:01:19.288 --> 00:01:22.082
And so began the war,

8
00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:27.254
a war that ravaged our planet
until it was consumed by death,

9
00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:32.467
and the Cube was lost
to the far reaches of space.

10
00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:38.890
<i>We scattered across the galaxy,
hoping to find it and rebuild our home,</i>

11
00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:43.478
searching every star, every world.

12
00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:49.943
And just when all hope seemed lost,
message of a new discovery drew us

13
00:01:50.027 --> 00:01:54.489
to an unknown planet called Earth.

14
00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.790
But we were already too late.

15
00:02:17.262 --> 00:02:19.306
Oh, God, five months of this.

16
00:02:19.389 --> 00:02:21.933
I can't wait to get a little taste of home.

17
00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:24.061
A plate of mama's alligators étfouffée.

18
00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:26.313
You've been talking
about barbecued 'gators and crickets

19
00:02:26.396 --> 00:02:29.608
for the last two weeks. I'm never going
to your mama's house, Fig. I promise.

20
00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.986
But Bobby, Bobby, 'gators are known
to have the most succulent meat.

21
00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:34.154
I understand.

22
00:02:37.783 --> 00:02:40.035
English, please. English.

23
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.830
I mean, how many times have we...
We don't speak Spanish. I told you that.

24
00:02:43.914 --> 00:02:46.166
Why you got to ruin it for me, man?
That's my heritage.

25
00:02:48.919 --> 00:02:50.170
Go with the Spanish. Whatever.

26
00:02:50.253 --> 00:02:55.342
Hey, you guys remember weekends?
Huh? The Sox at Fenway.

27
00:02:55.384 --> 00:02:58.095
-Cold hot dog and a flat beer.
-Perfect day.

28
00:02:59.137 --> 00:03:02.182
What about you, Captain?
You got a perfect day?

29
00:03:02.474 --> 00:03:05.310
I just can't wait to hold my baby girl
for the first time.

30
00:03:05.352 --> 00:03:06.895
-He's adorable.
-That's too...

31
00:03:06.978 --> 00:03:07.979
Shut up!

32
00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:28.875
Hey, I'm ready to do this.

33
00:03:29.292 --> 00:03:31.878
Hey, any of y'all grow some balls,
come see me on the court, man.

34
00:03:31.962 --> 00:03:33.088
Hey, hey!

35
00:03:36.091 --> 00:03:37.384
Watch this crossover, baby.

36
00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:40.679
Like Jordan in his prime,
pushing through the front line.

37
00:03:41.471 --> 00:03:43.682
-Step aside, ladies.
-Oh, man. What?

38
00:03:43.724 --> 00:03:46.059
-Lennox!
-Hey, what are you doing?

39
00:03:46.101 --> 00:03:48.729
-Water?
-Thank you.

40
00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:50.397
Are you gonna help me with the gear?

41
00:03:57.529 --> 00:04:01.908
Colonel Sharp, we have an inbound
unidentified infiltrator, 10 miles out.

42
00:04:06.621 --> 00:04:11.126
Unidentified aircraft, you are
in restricted US military airspace.

43
00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:14.045
Squawk ident and proceed east
out of the area.

44
00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:19.968
Raptors one and two, snap to heading
two-five-zero to intercept.

45
00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:22.929
Bogie is in the weeds ten miles out,
not squawking.

46
00:04:27.601 --> 00:04:32.564
Unidentified aircraft, we will escort you
to US SOCCENT airbase.

47
00:04:32.606 --> 00:04:35.776
If you do not comply,
we will use deadly force.

48
00:04:37.611 --> 00:04:39.112
Copy the bogie.

49
00:04:39.154 --> 00:04:41.782
Tail 4500 X-ray.

50
00:04:41.823 --> 00:04:44.326
Sir, says here 4500 X
was shot down three months ago.

51
00:04:44.409 --> 00:04:46.286
-Afghanistan.
-That's got to be a mistake.

52
00:04:46.328 --> 00:04:48.455
-Check again, then recheck.
- I did, sir.

53
00:04:48.497 --> 00:04:50.499
A friend of mine was on that chopper.

54
00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:55.128
Unidentified aircraft, we will escort you
to US SOCCENT airbase.

55
00:04:57.297 --> 00:05:00.550
-Radar, where's the inbound?
-Bogie's five miles out, sir.

56
00:05:04.054 --> 00:05:05.806
-My wife on?
-Yes, Captain.

57
00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:09.810
-My ladies!
-Look.

58
00:05:10.101 --> 00:05:11.895
Oh, my goodness. Look at her.
She's getting so big.

59
00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:15.148
Look at those cheeks.
I just wanna chew on them.

60
00:05:15.398 --> 00:05:16.983
Baby, we made a good-looking kid.

61
00:05:17.025 --> 00:05:19.194
I know that people say that all the time,

62
00:05:19.277 --> 00:05:21.655
but, wow, we made
one good-looking kid. Nice work.

63
00:05:21.738 --> 00:05:23.532
She has your laugh.

64
00:05:23.615 --> 00:05:26.243
-She laughed?
-Her first one, yeah.

65
00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:29.287
You sure she didn't just fart?

66
00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:31.164
No, she's a lady.

67
00:05:31.248 --> 00:05:33.792
She doesn't know you yet, but she will.

68
00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:38.171
4500 X. Something's not right.

69
00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:44.928
Bogie's on the deck.

70
00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:48.181
Whoa, whoa, whoa. Radar's jammed.

71
00:05:49.641 --> 00:05:51.518
It's coming from the chopper.

72
00:05:51.893 --> 00:05:54.187
-Will?
-Sarah?

73
00:05:54.271 --> 00:05:58.149
Hey, Sarah, if you can hear me,
I love you and I'll be home soon.

74
00:06:01.194 --> 00:06:03.864
-To the right. Go to the right.
-Check fire.

75
00:06:07.659 --> 00:06:10.912
MH-53 pilot, power down now.

76
00:06:14.749 --> 00:06:17.294
Have your crew step out
or we will kill you.

77
00:06:26.261 --> 00:06:29.139
Hold your fire! Stand by to engage!

78
00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:37.397
My God.

79
00:06:53.997 --> 00:06:57.667
They bombed the antenna farm!
We're under attack!

80
00:07:25.487 --> 00:07:27.864
Go! Move! Move!

81
00:07:34.788 --> 00:07:36.498
It's going after the files!

82
00:07:36.581 --> 00:07:37.958
Cut the hard lines!

83
00:07:38.041 --> 00:07:40.001
I need a key! It's locked!

84
00:07:41.836 --> 00:07:43.546
Move! Move!

85
00:07:48.802 --> 00:07:51.262
Here, come here! Come here!

86
00:08:06.569 --> 00:08:08.321
Here, hide in here!

87
00:08:08.613 --> 00:08:10.490
Oh, my God. Okay.

88
00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:13.159
No!

89
00:08:14.953 --> 00:08:15.954
Oh!

90
00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:27.757
What the...

91
00:08:36.599 --> 00:08:38.476
Epps, let's go!

92
00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:00.915
Okay, Mr Witwicky, you're up.

93
00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:03.668
Sorry, I got a lot of stuff.

94
00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:04.961
Watch.

95
00:09:05.045 --> 00:09:06.046
Okay.

96
00:09:07.464 --> 00:09:08.757
For my family...

97
00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:13.303
Who did... Who did that?
People! Responsibility.

98
00:09:16.556 --> 00:09:17.557
Okay.

99
00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:20.727
So, for my family genealogy report,

100
00:09:20.769 --> 00:09:23.938
I decided to do it
on my great-great-grandfather,

101
00:09:24.272 --> 00:09:27.275
who was a famous man,
Captain Archibald Witwicky.

102
00:09:27.692 --> 00:09:28.777
Very famous explorer.

103
00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:35.325
In fact, he was one of the first to explore
the Arctic Circle, which is a big deal.

104
00:09:37.118 --> 00:09:42.624
In 1897, he took 41 brave sailors
straight into the Arctic Shelf.

105
00:09:43.541 --> 00:09:47.754
Move faster, men! Move! Chop! Heave!

106
00:09:47.879 --> 00:09:53.259
The ice is freezing faster than
it's melting! Chop faster! Heave, men!

107
00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:58.181
Heave! No sacrifice, no victory!

108
00:09:58.264 --> 00:10:00.683
We'll get to the Arctic Circle, lads!

109
00:10:01.601 --> 00:10:03.895
So that's the story, right?

110
00:10:03.937 --> 00:10:06.189
And here we have some of
the basic instruments and tools

111
00:10:06.272 --> 00:10:08.108
used by 19th-century seamen.

112
00:10:09.442 --> 00:10:11.444
This here is the quadrant,
which you can get for 80 bucks.

113
00:10:11.486 --> 00:10:12.821
It's all for sale, by the way.

114
00:10:12.904 --> 00:10:17.617
Like the sextant here.
$50 for this, which is a bargain.

115
00:10:17.784 --> 00:10:20.203
These are pretty cool.
These are my grandfather's glasses.

116
00:10:20.286 --> 00:10:23.039
I haven't quite gotten them appraised
yet, but they've seen many cool things.

117
00:10:23.123 --> 00:10:25.375
Are you going to sell me his liver?

118
00:10:25.458 --> 00:10:28.086
Mr Witwicky, this isn't show and sell.
It's the 11th grade.

119
00:10:28.128 --> 00:10:29.295
I don't think your grandfather would be

120
00:10:29.337 --> 00:10:30.630
particularly proud of what you're doing.

121
00:10:30.672 --> 00:10:33.967
I know. I'm sorry. I just, you know,
this is all going towards my car fund.

122
00:10:34.008 --> 00:10:36.136
You can tell your folks. It's on eBay.
I take PayPal.

123
00:10:36.177 --> 00:10:37.554
Cold hard cash works, too.

124
00:10:37.637 --> 00:10:39.973
And the compass makes a great gift
for Columbus Day.

125
00:10:40.014 --> 00:10:41.391
-Sam!
-Sorry.

126
00:10:41.599 --> 00:10:43.309
Unfortunately,
my great-great-grandfather,

127
00:10:43.393 --> 00:10:48.273
the genius that he was, wound up
going blind and crazy in a psycho ward,

128
00:10:48.314 --> 00:10:49.524
drawing these strange symbols

129
00:10:49.607 --> 00:10:52.110
and babbling on about
some giant ice man

130
00:10:52.152 --> 00:10:53.862
that he thought he'd discovered.

131
00:10:53.945 --> 00:10:57.824
Okay. Might be a pop quiz tomorrow.
Might not.

132
00:10:57.866 --> 00:11:00.201
-Sleep in fear tonight.
-Here, you want? Here, 50. 40? 307

133
00:11:00.285 --> 00:11:02.662
-Sam?
-Yeah. Sorry, sorry.

134
00:11:02.704 --> 00:11:05.331
Okay. Pretty good, right?

135
00:11:06.708 --> 00:11:09.669
I'd say a solid B-.

136
00:11:11.337 --> 00:11:12.338
A B-7?

137
00:11:12.422 --> 00:11:14.507
You were hawking
your great-grandfather's crap

138
00:11:14.549 --> 00:11:15.758
-in my classroom.
-No, kids enjoy...

139
00:11:15.842 --> 00:11:17.093
-Look, can you do me a favour?
-What?

140
00:11:17.177 --> 00:11:19.512
Can you look out the window
for a second? You see my father?

141
00:11:19.596 --> 00:11:21.181
-He's the guy in the green car.
-Yeah.

142
00:11:21.264 --> 00:11:23.933
Okay, I wanna tell you about a dream.
A boy's dream.

143
00:11:24.017 --> 00:11:25.810
And a man's promise to that boy.

144
00:11:25.852 --> 00:11:28.730
He looked at me in the eye.
He said, "Son, I'm gonna buy you a car.

145
00:11:28.813 --> 00:11:31.107
"But I want you to bring me $2,000
and three As."

146
00:11:31.191 --> 00:11:33.359
Okay? I got the 2,000 and I got two As.

147
00:11:33.401 --> 00:11:37.530
Okay? Here's the dream. Your B-.
Dream gone. Kaput.

148
00:11:38.364 --> 00:11:42.410
Sir, just ask yourself,
what would Jesus do?

149
00:11:43.828 --> 00:11:45.163
Yes! Yes, yes.

150
00:11:46.664 --> 00:11:47.665
So?

151
00:11:47.957 --> 00:11:48.958
A-. It's an A, though.

152
00:11:49.042 --> 00:11:50.835
Wait, wait, wait. I can't see. It's an A.

153
00:11:50.877 --> 00:11:52.629
-So I'm good?
-You're good.

154
00:11:53.379 --> 00:11:56.633
- I got a little surprise for you, son.
-What kind of...

155
00:11:56.925 --> 00:11:58.968
Yeah, a little surprise.

156
00:11:59.969 --> 00:12:03.598
No. No, no, no, no. Dad!

157
00:12:04.557 --> 00:12:06.059
